course: dictionary
lesson: 12413
title: Dampness: a little wet
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Dampness is a slight wetness. It is a little wet, and it is often unpleasant, like a cold, soggy towel. «The walls of the cellar felt cold, with a sour dampness.» The word cellar tells you it is an underground room, and the words cold and sour show the dampness is not nice.
explain: Dampness can also mean wet air or wet walls inside a building. «Black mould grew in the corner of the bedroom because of the dampness.» Mould is a furry growth that smells bad, and it can make it hard to breathe. This is why grown-ups fix damp walls.
explain: Ask yourself what the word is doing in the sentence. Is something wet a little, or is a building wet? Then swap in your meaning, such as «the wet, unpleasant feeling», and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Dampness is a noun, and it is uncountable: we say some dampness, not a dampness.
fact: «Dampness» means slight wetness, often in an unpleasant way.
fact: In buildings, dampness means wet air or wet walls, which can cause mould and smell.
fact: Science lessons use dampness for water in the air (humidity) and water in materials.
fact: In stories, «the dampness of the cellar» creates a cold, gloomy setting.
exercise: «The walls of the cellar felt cold, with a sour dampness.» What is dampness here? | *Slight wetness, often unpleasant | Bright sunshine | Dry, dusty air | A loud noise
exercise: «Black mould grew in the corner because of the dampness.» What did the dampness probably cause? | A warm fire | Loud music | Sparkling new paint | *Mould on the walls
exercise: Which phrase means a problem with dampness in a building? | A warm bath | A bright star | A sunny day | *rising damp
test: «Mum said the dampness in the bathroom could make people cough.» Why is dampness bad here? | *It can help mould grow, which is bad for breathing | It makes the room too bright | It stops the room from smelling | It makes the walls shiny
test: In a story, what setting can dampness create? | A bright, happy party | A noisy market | A hot desert | *A cold, gloomy setting
exam: Which sentence uses dampness with the right meaning? | The dampness of the bright sun made everyone cheer. | *The dampness of the cellar made the air heavy and cold. | She ran dampness up the hill to school. | The dampness shouted across the playground.
